  ('5f88fb9a-6aab-5e11-b416-3ff853097db1', 'en-ak9-mastare-idiom', 'Engelska', 'ak9', 'Idiom', 'Mästarprov: Idiom', 'Blandade frågor ur hela området, i ny ordning varje gång. Klarar du det med minst två stjärnor sitter området.', 12, 'mastare', null),
  ('794f780a-0a7d-5a90-8e1c-da015ddc6163', 'en-ak9-lasforstaelse-1', 'Engelska', 'ak9', 'Läsförståelse', 'The Repair Café', 'En artikel om ett kafé där man lagar trasiga saker, med frågor om vad som står och vad Hanna menar.', 13, 'vanlig', 'Every second Saturday, the old library in Norrby becomes a workshop. People arrive with broken toasters, torn jackets, bikes with flat tyres and lamps that no longer work. They do not come to buy anything new. They come to get their things repaired – for free.

The Repair Café was started two years ago by Hanna Lind, who was fifteen at the time. “I had a laptop that stopped charging,” she explains. “The shop said a repair would cost more than a new computer. My grandfather looked at it for ten minutes and found a loose cable. That made me angry, in a good way. How many things are thrown away because nobody knows how easy they are to fix?”

Hanna asked her grandfather and two of his retired friends if they wanted to help. On the first Saturday, eight people came. Today there are often more than fifty visitors, and fifteen volunteers, most of them pensioners and teenagers.

The rules are simple. Visitors must stay while their things are being repaired, and they are encouraged to help. “The goal is not only to fix things,” says Hanna. “It’s to show people that they can do it themselves next time.”

Not everything can be saved. Some machines are built so that they cannot be opened without breaking them. “Those are the ones that make me angry in a bad way,” Hanna laughs.

Last year the café repaired more than 600 items. Hanna has kept a list of every single one.'),
  ('b4ffc932-a109-5ed7-abec-72a821e9e719', 'en-ak9-lasforstaelse-2', 'Engelska', 'ak9', 'Läsförståelse', 'The notes', 'En berättelse om hemliga lappar i ett skåp, med frågor om vad som står och vad man kan förstå mellan raderna.', 14, 'vanlig', 'Every morning for three weeks, Leo had found a note in his locker. The notes were short and written in neat blue ink. Nice goal yesterday. Good luck on the maths test. Your drawing in the corridor is brilliant.

At first he thought it was a joke. His friends were the type who would do something like that and then laugh at him for a month. But none of them could draw a straight line, let alone write in handwriting that neat, and none of them had been at the match.

He started to arrive earlier to see who put them there. On Monday he came at half past seven, but the note was already in the locker. On Tuesday he came at seven. The note was there. On Wednesday the school was still locked when he arrived, and when the caretaker opened the doors at a quarter to seven, Leo ran to his locker. The note was waiting for him.

“Stop trying so hard,” it said. “You will find out on Friday.”

On Friday there was no note. Instead, at lunch, a girl from the year below sat down opposite him. Leo recognised her from the art room: she was always the last to leave.

“Your locker used to be mine,” she said. “The lock is broken. It opens from the back, through the storage room.” She pushed a small blue pen across the table. “I thought someone should tell you that people notice you.”'),
